# The machine-local roots. NO trailing slash on any of them, deliberately: a
# trailing slash means "directory only", and git treats a symlink as a blob, not
# a directory — so /data/ would NOT ignore data, which is a symlink into iCloud.
# Untracked, it would offer git an absolute path naming one machine. Which roots
# are symlinks is a per-machine fact (data is one by design, ext holds links, tmp
# is a real dir), so no slash on any: without one a rule ignores a directory AND
# a symlink, and stops depending on the shape a root happens to have on whoever's
# disk. tmp/ knowingly reuses the name of the retired swap space: it is now a
# real root (cache/ + logs/), disposable as ever.
/data
/tmp
/ext

# the four-root migration's transitional workspace (src/restructure/): manifests,
# dry-run worktree, reports. Delete this rule when both rooms have applied and
# src/restructure/ is dismantled.
/swap

# TRANSITIONAL: the OLD roots, still on disk (and still machine-local) until a
# room's apply_moves retires them. Between merge and apply a machine honestly
# holds both layouts; without these rules the old corpus floods git status and
# the xref scan (whose skip-roots derive from this file). Delete these four
# rules with /swap above, once both rooms have applied.
/input
/cache
/output
/logs

**/.DS_Store
__pycache__/

# the machine binding: this machine names ITSELF here, and that name is never
# shared, never transported. It sits at the root beside the other machine-local
# entries above (data/, tmp/, ext/) because that is what it IS — not inside
# rsc/machine/, where its name keys a registry every clone shares.
/machine-name.txt
